  "verbalization": "Atracurium, Pancuronium, and Vecuronium are all members of the neuromuscular nondepolarizing blockade class, which is characterized by their ability to inhibit acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction. This class of drugs binds to the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, leading to muscle relaxation. The shared relationship between these drugs and Chrnb1 suggests a common mechanism of action involving the nicotinic receptor, with Chrnb1 likely playing a role in the binding or modulation of these compounds. The lowest common ancestor of Atracurium, Pancuronium, and Vecuronium is the Ester class, indicating a shared structural feature that may contribute to their pharmacological effects. The evidence supports a direct interaction between these drugs and Chrnb1, likely through the nicotinic receptor, and underscores the importance of the nicotinic antagonist class in the pharmacology of neuromuscular blockade.",
